A||||4||fierosound||shifter problems - any ideas||||1||
Z||000000||fierosound||03-24-2001||04:55 AM||||Got a problem with a notchy shifter action in an 87 V6 w/Getrag 5-speed.  The transmission has just been professionally rebuilt by a reputable trans shop - lots of expensive parts (thank goodness our GM dealer gives our club a discount).  The clutch system is fairly new, and works, so the clutch releases properly.  After reinstalling thr trans and cradle, I adjusted the cables as per the instructions in the Helms manual.  Everything should work as new, right?<P>I seem to get a slight "hangup" between 1st & 2nd sometimes.  Also, when shifting into 3rd, it almost feels as if the trans has got a bad synchro.  This is what prompted the rebuild in the first place.  It's still there with new synchros.  What's the problem?  Stretched shift cable?  Worn out mechanisn in the console?<P>I thought I'd jog your minds - trying to avoid taking it to a dealer!<BR><p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 03-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Bob Englert||03-24-2001||06:43 AM||||You might want to verify with the rebuilder that they used GM Syncromesh transmission fluid or 5W-30 motor oil in the transmission.  On one of my 87 GT's it would be very hard to shift out of one gear and into another.  It would feel like it was sticking in gear.  I couple of weeks ago I drained the trans. oil and refilled with the GM Syncromesh.  The cars shift perfectly now!<P>I think the transmission had 90W gear oil instead of the proper 5W-30 motor oil.  The oil oil smells like gear oil.  I suspect that the much thicker gear oil was preventing the car from shifting smoothly.<P>I am very happy that I don't appear to have major internal trans problems that I was fearing before I changed the fluid.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||03-24-2001||08:33 AM||||Getrag... It seems they ALL do that. <P>Don't know if you're on Kreb's list, but there was a big discussion on the Getrag tranny, and all the problems people are having/have had with them, even on extremely low mileage units. Most dealt with selecting gears, grinding, notchyness, etc.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 03-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierosound||03-24-2001||03:47 PM||||The tranny came back empty, as I was doing the install myself anyway.  I used GM synchromesh tranny fluid and also added a bottle of Limited Slip Additive too.  I had a chance to do some more driving today and get the trans really warmed up.  It seems to be getting better.  Perhaps everything is just tight and it's taking awhile to "break in"...  Thanks guys||||reg||10
Z||000004||TheFiero64||03-26-2001||12:08 PM||||I would try this. I use it. It made it shift like butter. It is great stuff.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.redlineoil.com/redlineoil/mtlti.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.redlineoil.com/redlineoil/mtlti.htm</A>  I just ordered some. It seems like the only thing we should be using.<BR>You can order it here <A HREF="http://www.racerpartswholesale.com/redline4.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.racerpartswholesale.com/redline4.htm</A>  <P><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg"> <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||10
